  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA large-scale area of low pressure is currently moving through \ncentral Ontario. Remnant showers and storms from overnight \nmesoscale convective systems (MCS) continue across the region \nearly this morning in the unzipping warm sector. Morning \nconvection has complicated but not eliminated our risk of severe\nweather across the region. It has also acted to raise our short\nterm flood threat. \n\nOur morning sounding only measured a PWAT of 1.32 but continued\nmoisture advection from our west will look to advect PWATs near\n1.9 inches (just below the daily maximum of 1.96 inches) across\nthe region. This accompanied with a relatively high freezing \nlevel will allow for efficient warm rain processes in a very \nmoist environment. Instantaneous rainfall rates up to 3-6 inches\nper hour seem possible but this will be tempered by shorter \nresidence time of these rates owing to quick storm motions. With\nmore progressive storms we have a higher chance of exceeding \nour 3 hour flash flood guidance more so than our 1 hour values \nlikely requiring multiple rounds of storms. Morning rain is \nacting to set the table for flood issues later across a wider \nportion of the region and thus our Flash Flood Watch has \nexpanded one tier of counties further north. The Flood Watch \ncontinues across the region through 11pm. \n\nSeveral factors in our previous analysis remain true: we have a\nvery intense (for July) left exit jet region overhead, dew \npoints are in the low 70s and shear across the region is even \nover performing expectations with mesoscale analysis suggesting \nupwards of 50 knots available in the 0-6km layer. The locally \nlacking ingredient is instability due to widespread rain across \nthe region this morning. However, a local lack of instability \ndoesn't end our severe threat if we can produce instability to \nour west and advect storms into the region. This is likely the \nway our severe threat is shifting. If storms can form to our \nwest and move into our incredibly strong dynamic environment \nthen we still have a very high end severe threat on the table. \nIf storms do not form west of us and move in then lower level \nstorms can continue going forward and we may not realize the \nfull potential of our environment for today. To this point there\nis a large area of clearing and Cu popping across IN and \nwestern OH. This activity is already even or south of Pittsburgh\nand any activity across our region from this likely focuses our\nsevere threat Pittsburgh on south, especially along the I-70 \ncorridor. \n\nThe amount of shear available in the environment points towards\nthe possibility of Supercellular or QLCS structures. Each of \nthese storm modes can bring damaging wind gusts and tornadoes. \nMorning rain has acted to locally lower LCLs nodding towards a \npossibly improving Tornado threat. Going along with this is \ndrastically improving structure of our hodographs this afternoon\nespecially I-70 on south and a noticeable uptick in our SIGTOR \nparameter. A Severe Thunderstorm Watch has been issued until 8pm\nfor all of our OH and WV counties and all of our PA counties \nsouth of a Beaver- Westmoreland line. \n\nExpect lingering showers and non-severe thunderstorms into \nearly Wednesday before a cold front tracks through the region.\n\nThe potential for precipitation remain low through the \nremainder of the work week and into the weekend before showers \nand storms again look possible on Sunday.",
